{
  "id": "Compound::DB00788 - Gene::1559",
  "verbalization": "Naproxen is implicated in the inhibition of Cyp2c9 through multiple mechanistic pathways, as evidenced by its role in causing pseudoporphyria, infertility in females, and subcutaneous nodules. These effects are linked to the binding of Naproxen to Cyp2c9, which is a key enzyme in the cytochrome P450 system. The lowest common ancestors of these relationships—Cyclooxygenase Inhibitor, Nonsteroidal Antiinflammatory Drug, and Benzenoid Aromatic Compound—suggest a shared biochemical profile, reinforcing the notion of a direct interaction between Naproxen and Cyp2c9. The evidence supports a mechanism where Naproxen, as an organic aromatic compound, inhibits Cyp2c9, potentially altering metabolic pathways involving the enzyme. The consistent presence of Cyp2c9 as a target across different pathological outcomes underscores the importance of this interaction in the context of drug action.",
